# Probe 34 (scripts/probe_map_scatter_card.py): candidate scatterChart, multiRowCard and map visuals, the three types real reports use that no contract covers.
# Seen (bridge captures, then a real refresh + Ctrl+S): multiRowCard with one measure drew "500 Total Amount"; with two measures drew both side by side in one row; with a column beside a measure drew one row per value (FR 200, UK 300) — so a column in Values groups the card, which is the type's point. scatterChart drew in all three forms: minimal (Category Country, X Total Amount, Y Target Amount), with Series = Region and Size = Total Amount (four sized, coloured bubbles with a legend), and with a drill-down Category. The two map visuals first drew Desktop's "Map and filled map visuals are disabled. To enable them, go to File > Options and settings > Options > Global > Security." — a Desktop-wide setting, off by default in this install, that stops Desktop from ever reading the shape. With it switched on they drew Bing maps: a bubble over the UK and one over France sized by Total Amount, and the Series variant split each bubble into Region segments with a legend — so Desktop geocoded "FR"/"UK" from the column NAME alone, with no dataCategory in the model. After the refresh the maps showed the disabled message again although the setting was still on (it appears to need a Desktop restart to stick). The save kept every projection as written and added exactly one thing: "active": true on the scatter chart's X projection, on all three scatters — which both sample files also carry, so it is Desktop's convention for that well and not drill view state. It also raised each changed file's $schema 2.13.0 -> 2.14.0, added its usual condition-less auto filterConfig, rewrote pages.json activePageName, and wrote settings.customMemoryLimit / customTimeoutLimit / queryLimitOption into report.json (from the user's visit to the Options dialog, not from anything Blueprint wrote). All content invented.

